<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0"><dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key><true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key><true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.screen-recording</key><true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key><true/>
  <!--
    Researched-but-unresolved question (added alongside the macOS
    ScreenCaptureKit system-audio helper, macos/sck-audio-capture.swift):
    does capturing system audio via ScreenCaptureKit from a spawned helper
    process need any entitlement beyond what is already declared above?

    This file has no `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` key, i.e. Cap does not
    appear to run under the App Sandbox, so the App-Sandbox-specific screen
    capture entitlement some sandboxed apps declare should not be required
    here — non-sandboxed, hardened-runtime apps gate ScreenCaptureKit purely
    through the TCC "Screen Recording" permission grant (the same one this
    app's existing AVFoundation-based screen capture already requires), not
    through an entitlement. That reasoning was NOT verified against a real
    Xcode/macOS build; it is documentation-only, and it does not address
    whether TCC attributes that grant to a helper binary spawned as a child
    process the same way it does to Cap.app itself, which is exactly the
    helper's architecture (see the "Requirements this helper cannot satisfy
    on its own" section in sck-audio-capture.swift). Verify on real hardware
    before shipping; do not assume this file is correct as-is.
  -->
</dict></plist>
